commit 1cfec8ad191e08cda577d6d5d4d01a3f2c48e4ca
Author: t <t@t>
Date:   Sat Aug 15 12:25:55 2026 -0400

    progress: emit a wall-clocked event stream so a stuck stage is visible
    
    A review that hangs mid-stage looked identical to a slow one: the
    only lines written were pass completions, so a hung falsify call
    produced silence until the 1200-2400s timeout fired. Measured
    2026-08-15, one run sat 24 minutes like that.
    
    Every phase transition now writes one stderr line prefixed with the
    wall clock since run start: run start/done, pass start (completion
    lines unchanged), per-finding falsify calls with dispositions, and
    the mutation baseline. The clock makes the gap between the last
    event and now directly readable, which is what turns "stuck" from a
    guess into an observation. stderr is the shared channel: the MCP
    server already tails it for forge_job_status, and the CLI already
    writes pass summaries there, so both entry points get the stream.
    
    Each emit flushes; stderr into a file is block-buffered, and an
    event that never leaves the buffer hides the stage it describes.
    
    Signed-off-by: Minxi Hou <houminxi@gmail.com>

